# Propagation of Silencing: Recruitment and Repression of Naive Chromatin In trans by Polycomb Repressed Chromatin

## abstract
The Polycomb group (PcG) proteins maintain stable and heritable repression of homeotic genes. Typically, Polycomb response elements (PRE) that direct PcG repression are located at great distances (10s of kb) from the promoters of PcG-repressed genes, and it is not known how these PREs can communicate with promoters over such distances. Using Class II mouse PRC core complexes (mPCCs) assembled from recombinant subunits, we investigated how PcG complexes might bridge distant chromosomal regions. Like native and recombinant Drosophila Class II complexes, mPCC represses chromatin remodeling and transcription. Interestingly, mPCC bound to one polynucleosome template can recruit a second template from solution and renders it refractory to transcription and chromatin remodeling. A Drosophila PRC core complex (dPCC) also is able to recruit a second template. Posterior sex combs (PSC), a subunit of dPCC, inhibits chromatin remodeling and transcription efficiently but requires assembly with dRING1 to recruit chromatin. Thus, repression and template bridging require different subunits of PcG complexes, suggesting that long-range effects may be mechanistically distinct from repression.

## Summary
The Polycomb group (PcG) proteins maintain stable and heritable repression of homeotic genes. Typically, Polycomb response elements (PRE) that direct PcG repression are located at great distances (10s of kb) from the promoters of PcG-repressed genes, and it is not known how these PREs can communicate with promoters over such distances. Using Class II mouse PRC core complexes (mPCCs) assembled from recombinant subunits, we investigated how PcG complexes might bridge distant chromosomal regions. Like native and recombinant Drosophila Class II complexes, mPCC represses chromatin remodeling and transcription. Interestingly, mPCC bound to one polynucleosome template can recruit a second template from solution and renders it refractory to transcription and chromatin remodeling. A Drosophila PRC core complex (dPCC) also is able to recruit a second template. Posterior sex combs (PSC), a subunit of dPCC, inhibits chromatin remodeling and transcription efficiently but requires assembly with dRING1 to recruit chromatin. Thus, repression and template bridging require different subunits of PcG complexes, suggesting that long-range effects may be mechanistically distinct from repression.
## Introduction
The Polycomb group (PcG) proteins are required to maintain stable and heritable repression of a number of genes, including the homeotic genes (for recent reviews see Francis and Kingston, 2001; Simon and Tamkun, 2002). Initially identified in Drosophila, the PcG genes are conserved in many organisms. In mammals, mutations or deletions of these genes cause homeotic transformations and disrupt cell differentiation and proliferation in the hematopoietic and nervous systems (Jacobs and van Lohuizen, 2002). Deregulated expression of PcG proteins has been correlated with various human cancers (Bea et al., 2001; Jacobs and van Lohuizen, 2002; Kleer et al., 2003; Raaphorst et al., 2000; Varambally et al., 2002; Vonlanthen et al., 2001).
In both Drosophila and humans, PcG proteins are found in at least two separate complexes. One complex (termed Class I) can methylate the tail of histone H3 (Cao et al., 2002; Czermin et al., 2002; Kuzmichev et al., 2002; Muller et al., 2002). The other class of complex (Class II) has been purified from Drosophila embryos (Polycomb repressive complex 1; dPRC1) and from HeLa cells (hPRC-H). The Drosophila Class II complex contains the PcG proteins Polycomb (Pc), Posterior sex combs (PSC), Polyhometic (Ph) and dRING1, and several other non-PcG proteins (Saurin et al., 2001; Shao et al., 1999). The HeLa complex contains human homologs of the PcG proteins found in dPRC1 but lacks most of its non-PcG subunits (Levine et al., 2002). In mammals, there are generally two or three homologs of each Drosophila PcG protein that can act synergistically or antagonistically and may mediate gene, tissue, and differentiation-stage specific effects (see Figure 1A, Satijn and Otte, 1999). For example, Bmi1 and Mel18, homologs of PSC, synergize to repress Hox genes (Akasaka et al., 2001) but act antagonistically in cell cycle regulation (Jacobs et al., 1999; Kanno et al., 1995).
In vitro, Class II PcG complexes block chromatin remodeling by hSWI/SNF (Francis et al., 2001; Levine et al., 2002; Shao et al., 1999) and repress transcription on a chromatin template (King et al., 2002). A core Class II complex containing only the four PcG subunits of the Drosophila native complex (Pc, Ph, PSC, and dRING1) can also repress remodeling and transcription.
In Drosophila, PcG transcriptional repression is targeted to specific genes by Polycomb response elements (PREs), which are binding sites for PcG proteins (Cao et al., 2002; Cavalli and Paro, 1998; Maurange and Paro, 2002; Orlando et al., 1998; Strutt et al., 1997). PcG proteins are most likely recruited to PREs through interactions with sequence-specific DNA binding proteins (for example, Busturia et al., 2001; Horard et al., 2000; Mishra et al., 2001; Mohd-Sarip et al., 2002; Mulholland et al., 2003), just as other chromatin modifying activities are targeted to enhancers and promoters by sequence specific transcriptional activators. PREs have not yet been characterized in mammals, although several DNA binding proteins that interact with PcG proteins have been identified (Atchison et al., 2003; Dahiya et al., 2001; Garcia et al., 1999; Ogawa et al., 2002; Satijn et al., 2001; Trimarchi et al., 2001).
PREs can be found many kilobases from the regulated promoter, implying PcG proteins can act across large regions of the genome. PREs are also located in the immediate vicinity of PcG repressed promoters, supporting the idea that interactions between distant and promoter-proximal PREs are important for repression (Bloyer et al., 2003; Ringrose et al., 2001). These interactions might occur through bridging of distal PcG-bound PREs, creating loops of intervening chromatin that are not bound by PcG proteins (Cavalli and Paro, 1998; Maurange and Paro, 2002; Orlando et al., 1998; Strutt et al., 1997). Alternatively, or in addition, PcG proteins might spread linearly along the template from the PREs to the repressed promoter.
To study how proximal and distal PREs might communicate to propagate repression, we tested whether class II PcG complexes are capable of bridging two separate chromatin templates. We have reconstituted a mouse PRC core complex (mPCC) that has the basic repressive abilities of other Class II complexes, and have used this new minimal complex in experiments that measure the ability of a template-bound complex to recruit a second template from solution. We show that both mPCC and the homologous Drosophila PRC core complex (dPCC) can recruit and repress a second chromatin template. These results demonstrate a novel capability of this class of PcG complexes that could be used to propagate PcG repression.
## Results
## Reconstitution and Characterization of Class II Mouse Core PcG Complexes
To compare mammalian Class II PcG proteins to Drosophila PcG proteins, we reconstituted mouse PRC core complexes (mPCCs) and tested their activities (Francis et al., 2001). We assembled mPCCs containing a single murine homolog of each subunit of dPCC. We chose mPh1, M33, Ring1A, and Bmi1 or Mel18 because they are abundant in most mammalian cells (Figure 1A). The subunits were coexpressed in Sf9 cells using baculoviruses, with one subunit carrying a Flag epitope tag (M33, Bmi1, or Mel18). The complex containing Bmi1 and purified using M33 flagged at its C terminus (Figure 1B, mPCC WT) was used in most experiments reported below and was representative of the other complexes tested. Other mPCCs were assembled with Mel18 replacing Bmi1 or with the Flag epitope on Bmi1 or Mel18 instead of M33. The choice of epitope-tagged subunit or PSC homolog does not significantly affect the stoichiometry of these complexes (see Supplemental Figure S1A at http://www.molecule.org/cgi/content/full/13/3/ 415/DC1).
Drosophila PCC binds to DNA nonspecifically (Francis et al., 2001), and this activity might be necessary for the PcG repression of remodeling and transcription. Filter binding was used to measure binding of mPCC to naked DNA. $K_{\mathsf{D}}$ values measured for the binding of mPCCs to a double stranded 155 bp DNA probe (between 0.05 and $0.15~\mathsf{n M}$ ) were similar to those measured for dPCC $(0.13\mathsf{n M})$ (Francis et al., 2001).
Drosophila PCC is able to bind to a chromatin template without altering its nuclease digestion pattern, though it does alter the sensitivity of the template to some nucleases (Francis et al., 2001). To determine the effect of mPCC on chromatin organization, we used a polynucleosome template made up of ten nucleosomes positioned on 5S sequences and two central unpositioned nucleosomes (“5S array,” see Figure 1C). When this template was treated with either MNase or DNase, a characteristic ladder indicative of the positioned nucleosomes is produced (Figure 1D). The nature of this ladder is not altered by prebinding mPCC WT (Figure 1D). However, MNase sensitivity is decreased and DNaseI sensitivity is enhanced.
These results mirror those seen with other mPCCs and with dPCC (not shown and Francis et al., 2001).
Using two different assays, we next tested whether mPCC could inhibit chromatin remodeling by hSWI/SNF complex on a polynucleosome template. First, we tested whether mPCC WT could prevent hSWI/SNF from randomizing the DNaseI or MNase cleavage pattern of a positioned nucleosomal array (Jaskelioff et al., 2000). Incubation of the 5S array with hSWI/SNF and ATP changed the characteristic “ladder” pattern of DNase I and MNase digestion, reflecting the remodeling of nucleosomes. When the template is preincubated with mPCC WT, hSWI/SNF was no longer able to randomize these patterns (Figure 1D). Thus, like dPCC, mPCC can block chromatin remodeling by hSWI/SNF. Second, we used a more quantifiable assay that measures the ability of a restriction enzyme to cleave the nucleosomal template. The central and unique HhaI site of the nucleosomal template (Figure 1C) was mostly inaccessible (about $20\%$ cleavage after 1 hr). Remodeling of the template by hSWI/SNF in the presence of ATP made the template highly accessible to HhaI (nearly $100\%$ cleavage) (Logie and Peterson, 1997). When the template was preincubated with mPCC WT, HhaI cutting was reduced to its unstimulated level. This also indicated that mPCC WT inhibits remodeling by hSWI/SNF (Figures 1E and 1F). All mPCC complexes tested inhibited remodeling similarly to dPCC. Half-maximal inhibition was achieved at a molar ratio of mPCC WT to nucleosomes of 0.5:1 (Figure 1F; Supplemental Figure S1C), using either 1 nM or 10 nM nucleosomes (Figure 1F; not shown). Complexes containing Mel18 instead of Bmi1 inhibited remodeling just as efficiently (Supplemental Figure S1C). Finally, we investigated whether mPCC WT could inhibit transcription from the 5S array template described above; this template contains the adenovirus E4 promoter and five Gal4 sites (Figure 1C). Efficient transcription of this template depends on the sequential addition of activator (Gal4-VP16) and RNA Pol II and general transcription factors (provided by HeLa nuclear extracts). The different mPCCs repressed transcription on this chromatin template at ratios to nucleosomes similar to those used to inhibit remodeling by hSWI/SNF (Figure 1G; data not shown). As observed for dPCC (King et al., 2002), repression required preincubation of mPCC with the array prior to the addition of HeLa nuclear extract.
The PSC and Ph subunits of dPCC are able to inhibit chromatin remodeling and transcription on their own (Francis et al., 2001; King et al., 2002). We tested whether individual subunits of mPCC (Bmi1, Mel18, mPh1, Ring1A, and M33) could inhibit remodeling and transcription. These subunits were purified and tested in the same functional assays used for the reconstituted complex (Supplemental Figures S1B and S1D). All subunits were individually at least 10-fold less efficient than mPCC at binding DNA, inhibiting hSWI/SNF activity, and inhibiting transcription. These results contrast with the observation that isolated Drosophila PSC is highly active in these assays (Francis et al., 2001).
