We found that recruitment of the 10N array was dependent on binding of mPCC WT to the bead-bound array. Up to $80\%$ of the 10N array was recruited, with a halftime of approximately 3 hr. The rate of recruitment increased when the concentration of mPCC initially incubated with the bead-bound 12N array was increased. There was a low level of mPCC-dependent recruitment of the small array to the beads alone, presumably caused by residual binding of mPCC to the beads (Figures 2B and 2C). No recruitment (less than $20\%$ ) was observed if (1) excess mPCC that is not bound to the array during the preincubation is left in the reaction by omitting the wash step or (2) both arrays are incubated at the same time with mPCC (data not shown; Figure 3A). Thus, recruitment did not appear to involve aggregation of templates with large amounts of PCC. Instead, this activity was best observed at low ratios of mPCC to template, suggesting that the process might require interactions between mPCC bound to the first template and a naive second template.
We then asked if this recruitment process was specific for a chromatin template or if a DNA fragment could also be recruited. A recruitment assay was performed at the same time on the 10N polynucleosome array and the DNA template used to assemble this array. The nucleosomal array was recruited more efficiently than the naked template (Figures 2D and 2E), suggesting that this mode of propagation of PcG repression favors regions covered by nucleosomes.
## The Polyhomeotic Subunit of mPCC Helps in Recruitment
We next asked whether particular subunits of mPCC were responsible for its recruitment activity. We reasoned that if specific subunits of mPCC must interact with chromatin or with other mPCC complexes to recruit chromatin from solution, then adding excess mPCC should block recruitment, because the 10N templates will be bound by free mPCC rather than mPCC that is bound to the 12N array. Similarly, an excess of a single subunit during the recruitment step might block or retard the process by occupying a binding site required for recruitment. We examined the kinetics of recruitment of the free 10N array template in the presence of an excess of mPCC, mPh1, M33, Bmi1, or Ring1A (50-fold over template) (Figure 3A). mPh1 blocked recruitment almost as effectively as intact mPCC, and M33 could also inhibit recruitment, albeit to a lesser degree than mPh1. Bmi1 and Ring1A had only a very weak effect on recruitment. These experiments suggest that M33 and mPh1 might be particularly important for recruitment of a free template.
To further test the role of mPh1 in recruitment, we prepared complexes lacking mPh1 (mPCCmPh1). This approach is not possible with M33, since this subunit is required to form a stable mPCC complex (data not shown). Removing mPh1 from mPCC had a small but reproducible effect on its ability to recruit another template (Figure 3B). In contrast, mPCCWT and mPCCmPh1 bound DNA and inhibited chromatin remodeling to similar degrees (Figure 3C). The lack of a complete defect in recruitment by mPCCmPh1 is consistent with a partially redundant role for M33 and mPh1 in recruitment (Figure 3A). Therefore, the ability of mPCCWT to inhibit remodeling and transcription and its ability to recruit a free nucleosomal array might be separable and might require different subunits.
## The Recruited Template Is Resistant to Remodeling and Is Refractory to Transcription
We next tested whether naive templates recruited by mPCC are also refractory to chromatin remodeling by hSWI/SNF and transcription. The REA assay was used to monitor inhibition of chromatin remodeling on both the bead-bound (12N) and free (10N) polynucleosome templates (Figure 4A). After various incubation times, both pellet and supernatant fractions were separated, and in turn split into three samples. One was left untreated (NC), one was cut by HhaI (H), and one was cut by HhaI in the presence of SWI/SNF and ATP $(H+S)$ . Samples were then deproteinized and resolved on agarose gels (Figure 4B). When the 12N template was incubated with buffer before the addition of the 10N template, no recruitment was observed and both templates can be remodeled by SWI/SNF (increase in cutting by HhaI in the presence of SWI/SNF). When the 12N template was incubated with mPCC before addition of the 10N template, recruitment of the 10N template was observed after 3 hr and nearly complete after 7 hr (NC sample, 10N uncut in pellet fraction P). At the beginning of the reaction, remodeling was inhibited only on the 12N template, but after 3 and 7 hr remodeling was also inhibited on the recruited 10N template.
We quantified the degree of inhibition of SWI/SNF remodeling on each template (Figure 4C) and found that the recruited fraction of the 10N template was refractory to remodeling while the nonrecruited fraction of 10N present in the same reaction could still be remodeled by SWI/SNF (right panel of Figure 4C). We also observed that recruitment did not decrease inhibition of remodeling on the bead-bound 12N template (left panel of Figure 4C). This indicates that there is no transfer of inhibition of chromatin remodeling from one template to another but that both “recruiting” and “recruited” templates become refractory to remodeling.
