## Drosophila PCC Also Recruits Nucleosomal Arrays from Solution
The ability of Class II PcG complexes to inhibit chromatin remodeling and transcription is conserved between mouse and Drosophila. If the ability to bridge two chromatin fibers is central to how PcG proteins mediate long-range repression, dPCC might also have the recruitment activity characterized for mPCC. We found that dPCC bound to a 12N array was able to recruit 10N arrays from solution (Figures 5A and 5B) while beads alone did not significantly recruit a free array. Recruitment occurred more slowly with dPCC than with mPCC (half-time of 6 hr).
A single subunit of dPCC, PSC, can bind DNA and inhibit chromatin remodeling and transcription almost as well as complete dPCC. This allowed us to test whether the other subunits of dPCC (Ph, Pc, and dRING1) were required for recruitment. We compared the abilities of PSC and dPCC to recruit a free array. PSC recruited nucleosomal arrays from solution less efficiently than dPCC when added at 15 nM (Figures 5A, 5B, and 5D). At higher concentrations $(45~\mathsf{n M})$ ), PSC could recruit a free array, but less efficiently than dPCC at a lower concentration $(15\mathsf{n M})$ (Figure 5C). Background binding of the free array to the beads alone was greater for PSC than for dPCC, particularly at high concentrations (Figure 5C, “beads” points at 6 and 24 hr), so recruitment by PSC might be overestimated in these assays. We conclude that under conditions where dPCC and PSC alone both inhibit chromatin remodeling with comparable efficiency (Figure 5D), PSC alone is at least three times less efficient at recruitment than dPCC.
This observation suggests that at least one other subunit is required for recruitment. To identify this protein or proteins, we prepared dPCC lacking either Pc or Ph or both of them (Figure 5E) and tested these subcomplexes in the recruitment and inhibition of chromatin remodeling assays. Complexes lacking Pc or Ph, or both, recruit free templates (Figures 5F–5H). Thus, PSC plus dRING1 is sufficient for recruitment and neither Pc nor Ph is essential for the process. It should be pointed out that a small contribution by Pc and/or Ph (as observed with mPCC) might not be apparent due to experimental variability. In fact the complex lacking Ph showed less recruitment in some experiments (Figure 5H). As expected from the finding that PSC alone strongly inhibits chromatin remodeling, all of the subcomplexes are comparable in this assay (Figure 5I). Thus, unlike inhibition of transcription and chromatin remodeling, which can be carried out by PSC alone, dRING1 is also required for recruitment. These results imply that chromatin binding activity (such as that mediated by PSC) is not sufficient to bridge two templates.
