sion. Class II PRC core complexes bound to a chromatin template are capable of recruiting and repressing a second naive chromatin template. Different subunits are required for recruitment and repression. dPSC is able to strongly repress remodeling and transcription, but addition of the dRING1 protein is needed to observe significant recruitment of a free array. The removal of the Ph protein from the mouse complex impairs recruitment but not repression. We infer that the recuitment and repression activities of PCCs represent distinct functions of Class II complexes that use distinct domains.
Recruitment of a free nucleosomal array might involve several mechanisms. It is possible that individual PcG complexes might have multiple binding sites for chromatin and might bind multiple arrays. Alternatively, each PcG complex might bind a single array, with recruitment mediated by interactions between PcG complexes. In this case, domains that mediate interactions among PcG proteins could play a key role in recruitment. Such domains are the SAM domain of Ph, which can form oligomers (Kim et al., 2002), or the Ring domain of Ring and Psc/Bmi1, which is responsible for interactions between these subunits (Kentsis and Borden, 2000). An excess of mPh1 inhibited recruitment, and deletion of this subunit from the mammalian complex impaired recruitment. However, Drosophila Ph is not necessary for recruitment by a minimal complex containing dPSC and dRING1. In this case, dRING1 might mediate dimerization of PSC, thereby bridging two PSC-bound templates. These results thus suggest that multiple domains in different PcG proteins might contribute to long-range chromatin interactions, and that changing the composition or conformation of PcG complexes could influence this activity. In vivo studies of long-range cis and trans interactions among PREs are consistent with this type of complexity since different PREs behave differently in these assays, and PREs that bind the same PcG proteins do not necessarily interact (e.g., Bantignies et al., 2003; Muller et al., 1999b).
Recruitment is a slow process relative to other PcG functions such as inhibition of chromatin remodeling. Covalent modifications of histones, like histone H3 methylation by Class I PcG complexes (Cao et al., 2002; Czermin et al., 2002; Kuzmichev et al., 2002; Muller et
