al., 2002), may influence the kinetics of recruitment. This process could also be regulated by additional proteins, like the GAGA protein in Drosophila, which can mediate interaction between separate templates (Mahmoudi et al., 2002). In vivo, the recruitment process might be favored by an enforced proximity between PREs and promoters, located in PcG bodies (Saurin et al., 1998). Finally, a speculative hypothesis is that recruitment requires a conformational change in the PcG complex that occurs slowly.
PcG proteins are believed to function across large $(100\mathsf{k b})$ regions in both mammals and Drosophila. While the location and even the existence of mammalian PREs remains speculative, it is known that Drosophila PREs can be located tens of kilobases apart in the same cluster. It is inferred that PcG proteins bound to these distal and promoter-proximal PREs must interact with each other. In reporter gene assays in flies, PREs have also been shown to mediate long-distance interactions both in cis and in trans–which seem to involve stable association of distal PREs (Bantignies et al., 2003; Muller et al., 1999b). The recruitment activity described here provides a mechanism by which distant PREs could be linked by PcG proteins without a requirement that PcG proteins coat the intervening sequences.
The recruitment activity characterized in vitro could also underlie two genetically characterized trans-interactions that can involve PREs: pairing sensitive silencing and transposon homing (reviewed in Kassis, 2002). In transposon homing, transgenes containing a PRE preferentially integrate at a site in the genome close to endogenous PREs (Hama et al., 1990; Kassis et al., 1992). Pairing sensitive silencing refers to the enhanced silencing observed when PRE repressed genes are integrated on each homologous chromosome or in two distal regions on the same chromosome (Muller et al., 1999a; Sigrist and Pirrotta, 1997) and requires long-distance interactions between these PREs, as recently visualized (Bantignies et al., 2003). Both phenomena might involve trans-interactions between PREs located at distal sights, and might involve interactions such as those described here between class II PcG complexes.
## Functional Comparison of Mammalian and Drosophila PcG Complexes
In addition to the characterization of a new activity of Class II PcG complexes, this paper also characterizes various mammalian core complexes and compares their basic activities with those of the Drosophila homolog complex dPCC. Although most of the assays presented in this study have been performed using a core complex containing mPh1, M33, Bmi1, and Ring1A, we found that the basic activities of mammalian Class II PcG complexes are not affected by changing the Pc or PSC homolog (Supplemental Figure S1). These data suggest that different variations of mammalian PcG complexes, which are observed in different cells at different times of differentiation, nevertheless have similar repressive activities. One difference observed between the Drosophila and mammalian PcG proteins is that while dPSC inhibits chromatin remodeling and transcription nearly as efficiently as the core complex, Bmi1 and Mel18 are weakly active in both assays. Perhaps phosphorylation of Bmi1 and Mel18 affects their activity, as has been observed in vivo (Voncken et al., 1999). Alternatively, a sequence corresponding to the large C-terminal domain of PSC, which is implicated in inhibition of remodeling (N.J.F. and I.F.G.K., unpublished data), is not present in Bmi1 or Mel18. It will therefore be important to determine what is responsible for the difference in activity between PSC and its mammalian homologs, and which subunits and protein domains are critical for the conserved activities of mPCC.
## Experimental Procedures
## Protein Expression and Purification
Flag epitope-tagged mPh1, M33, Bmi1, Mel18, and Ring1A were expressed in SF9 cells using the bac-to-bac system (Gibco) as described for their Drosophila homologs (Francis et al., 2001). Indicated subunits carried the Flag epitope tag. Proteins and complexes were bound to M2 anti-Flag beads in BC300, washed with up to 2 M KCl (BC2000), and eluted by with Flag peptide $(0.4~\mathrm{mg/ml})$ in BC300. All solutions contained $0.05\%$ NP40, 0.1 mM DTT, and a combination of protease inhibitors. hSWI/SNF was purified from a flag-tagged Ini1 HeLa cell line as described previously (Sif et al., 2001). Protein concentrations were determined by Bradford assay and purity was assessed using colloidal coomassie (Novagen) and silver stained gels.
## Polynucleosome Templates
The polynucleosome templates were assembled by gradient salt dialysis with HeLa core histones and three different DNA fragments. The 12N template is the Asp718-ClaI fragment described (Utley et al., 1998). The 10N template was obtained by deletion of two 5S repeats. The 13N template (Figure 5) is the same as 12N but also contains sequence from the Ubx promoter as described (Mulholland et al., 2003). To analyze transcriptional repression, a derivative of the 12N construct was used in which the sequence from $^{+28}$ to $+71$ after the major E4 start site had been duplicated. For DNaseI, Mnase, and REA assay, nucleosomes were assembled on the 12N fragment labeled at the Asp718 site with $\alpha^{32}{\mathsf{P d A T P}}$ (NEN). Transcription assays were performed on unlabeled polynucleosome templates. For recruitment assays, the recruiting template was assembled on the 10N or 12N fragment labeled at the Asp718 site with 32PdTTP and biotin-14-dATP and the recruited template was assembled on the 10N or 13N fragment labeled with 32PdTTP only. Concentration of 5S array refers to nucleosome concentration.
## Basic Assays to Characterize the Activity of mPCCs and Its Subunits
Double filter binding assays were performed as described (Francis et al., 2001). Concentrations of proteins or complexes refer to active concentrations, determined by filter binding (Francis et al., 2001). DNaseI, MNase digestion, and REA assays were performed as described in Francis et al. (2001) and transcription assays according to King et al. (2002).
## Recruitment Assay
The recruiting polynucleosome template, assembled on the 12N fragment and labeled with biotin-14-dATP (Gibco-BRL) and $\mathsf{s}_{2}\mathsf{p}$ -dTTP (NEN), was attached to streptavidin coated magnetic beads (dynabeads M280, Dynal) by incubating for $1\ \mathsf{h r}$ at $4^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in 300 mM KCl, $10\%$ glycerol, 2 mM MgCl2, $\mathsf{0.5~m M}$ DTT, and $100~\upmu\upmu/\upmu\upmu$ BSA at a ratio of 8 ng of array DNA per ${\upmu\mathsf{g}}$ of beads. Beads were blocked for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ at $4^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in the attachment buffer containing casein $(60~\mathrm{mg/ml})$ and PVP $(5~\mathrm{mg/ml})$ ). The bead-bound template was washed several times in the attachment buffer, resuspended in TE Low $10~\mathsf{m M}$ Tris $\left[\mathsf{p}\mathsf{H}\otimes\right]$ and $0.25~\mathsf{m M}$ EDTA), and was kept at $4^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . Templates were stored up to 2 days before use. Reactions were performed in $60~\mathsf{m M}~\mathsf{K C l}$ , $12\%$ glycerol, $2\mathsf{m M M g}({\mathsf{C l}})_{2}$ , 1 mM DTT, and $100~\upmu\mathfrak{g}/$ ml BSA as described in Francis et al. (2001) and King et al. (2002). The 12N template attached to the beads was incubated for $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ with an excess of $\mathtt{m P C C}$ , washed to remove unbound mPCC, and incubated with the labeled 10N polynucleosome template. Typically, 4 times less 10N array was added than 12N template initially attached to the beads. At different time points, the 10N and 12N templates associated with the beads (pellet) or not associated (supernatant) were separated, resolved on a $1\%$ agarose gel, and quantified by phosphorimager (Molecular Dynamics). Recruitment over time is given as the percentage of 10N template present in the pellet.
## Recruitment Assay followed by a Remodeling Assay
The pellet (P) and supernatant (S) of a recruitment assay were separated and split into three reactions: undigested (NC), HhaI digested (H), and HhaI digested in the presence of an excess of hSWI/SNF and ATP $(\mathsf{H}+\mathsf{S})$ . After 1 hr, the products were deproteinized and separated on an agarose gel and the fraction of template digested by HhaI in the presence or absence of SWI/SNF was quantified to assess remodeling.
Recruitment Assay followed by a Transcription Assay Assays were performed as for recruitment, with 3 ng free 12N array and ${\sim}7\$ ng bead-bound array. At different time points, aliquots were removed and in vitro transcription reactions performed as described (King et al., 2002). The two templates used in this assay differed by their length (12N and 10N) and the distance between the transcription start site and the primer used to quantify transcription. Transcription was analyzed on the entire reaction without separating supernatant and pellet.
